WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.029
Correct.

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.857
Another trap is the
phrase optically denser.

00:00:03.187 --> 00:00:07.680
That refers to refractive index, not
necessarily greater mass per unit volume.

00:00:08.102 --> 00:00:11.795
Optical behavior and ordinary
mass density are different ideas.

00:00:12.617 --> 00:00:12.797
That

00:00:12.797 --> 00:00:16.819
explains a straw looking bent at the
water surface and a pool appearing

00:00:16.823 --> 00:00:18.302
shallower than it really is.

00:00:19.061 --> 00:00:19.629
Yes.

00:00:19.931 --> 00:00:23.755
Your brain interprets the light as
coming along a straight path, though

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:25.477
it changed direction at the boundary.

00:00:26.008 --> 00:00:28.903
Never judge safe diving
depth by this appearance.

00:00:29.636 --> 00:00:32.628
What happens through a rectangular
glass block with parallel

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.056
faces with air on both sides?

00:00:35.894 --> 00:00:39.940
At oblique entry, the ray bends
towards the normal, then away at exit.

00:00:40.499 --> 00:00:45.112
The emerging ray is parallel to the
original direction but displaced sideways.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:46.627
Exactly.

00:00:46.939 --> 00:00:49.793
At normal incidence, there
is no sideways displacement.

00:00:50.098 --> 00:00:54.044
A prism has non-parallel faces,
so its emerging ray can have

00:00:54.046 --> 00:00:55.609
a different overall direction.

00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:58.002
Draw the shape before deciding the path

00:00:58.331 --> 00:01:00.628
A prism spreads white light into colors.

00:01:01.274 --> 00:01:03.747
Is the prism manufacturing
colors from nothing?

00:01:04.528 --> 00:01:04.835
No.

00:01:05.364 --> 00:01:08.829
White light already contains a
range of visible wavelengths.

00:01:09.294 --> 00:01:12.562
They refract by different amounts,
so the prism separates them.

00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:14.727
That is dispersion.

00:01:15.476 --> 00:01:16.008
Correct.

00:01:16.413 --> 00:01:18.247
In ordinary glass, violet

00:01:18.264 --> 00:01:19.792
is deviated more than red.

00:01:20.075 --> 00:01:26.426
We name red, orange, yellow, green,
blue, indigo, and violet, but the actual

00:01:26.427 --> 00:01:30.524
spectrum changes continuously rather
than forming seven separate boxes.

00:01:31.293 --> 00:01:35.717
Rainbows involve refraction and
internal reflection in water droplets.

00:01:36.184 --> 00:01:39.414
The sun is generally behind
the person seeing the rainbow.

00:01:40.174 --> 00:01:40.782
Yes.

00:01:41.082 --> 00:01:43.453
Now, a red object is lit with white light.

00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:45.065
Why does it look red?

00:01:45.803 --> 00:01:50.259
It reflects red wavelengths relatively
strongly and absorbs much of the rest.

00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:53.126
It does not necessarily
create its own red light.

00:01:53.906 --> 00:01:57.886
What if we illuminate that idealized
red object with only blue light?

00:01:58.712 --> 00:02:01.986
It looks dark because there is little
red light available to reflect,

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:03.792
and it absorbs much of the blue.

00:02:04.265 --> 00:02:08.334
Real materials may reflect a range,
so the exact appearance varies.

00:02:09.087 --> 00:02:10.174
Good qualification.

00:02:10.656 --> 00:02:13.503
A filter selectively
transmits some wavelengths and

00:02:13.503 --> 00:02:15.088
absorbs or reflects others.

00:02:15.405 --> 00:02:17.878
It does not simply paint
every passing ray its

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:18.468
own color.

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:24.007
A red filter followed by a blue filter
can transmit very little light if their

00:02:24.008 --> 00:02:25.986
transmission bands hardly overlap.

00:02:26.742 --> 00:02:28.973
Now mix red and green light.

00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:30.056
What do we get?

00:02:30.758 --> 00:02:31.553
Yellow light.

00:02:31.958 --> 00:02:35.918
Green plus blue gives cyan, and
red plus blue gives magenta.

00:02:36.361 --> 00:02:38.983
Suitable amounts of all
three can produce white.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:40.566
Exactly.

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:42.730
This is additive color mixing.

00:02:43.338 --> 00:02:47.219
Mixing paints is different because
pigments absorb parts of the illumination.

00:02:47.692 --> 00:02:51.866
Many screens use red, green,
and blue sub-pixels to reproduce

00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:55.620
a broad range of colors, not
every physically possible color

00:02:56.052 --> 00:03:01.079
Our eye model has a cornea, pupil,
iris, lens, retina, and optic nerve.

00:03:01.451 --> 00:03:04.175
Which part is a hole,
not a muscle or a lens?

00:03:04.931 --> 00:03:05.681
The pupil.

00:03:06.197 --> 00:03:08.097
It is the opening that lets light in.

00:03:08.616 --> 00:03:12.205
The iris is the colored muscular
structure that controls its size.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:13.772
Exactly.

00:03:14.039 --> 00:03:16.571
The cornea provides much
of the eye's refraction.

00:03:16.907 --> 00:03:20.701
The lens adjusts focusing so a
clear image forms on the retina

00:03:20.902 --> 00:03:22.897
where light sensitive cells respond.

00:03:23.657 --> 00:03:26.032
The optic nerve carries
signals to the brain.

00:03:26.413 --> 00:03:26.554
The

00:03:26.557 --> 00:03:28.713
sclera is the tough protective outer

00:03:28.713 --> 00:03:29.124
layer.

00:03:29.846 --> 00:03:30.238
Good.

00:03:30.678 --> 00:03:32.907
The retinal image is real and inverted.

00:03:33.216 --> 00:03:35.231
The brain interprets visual signals.

00:03:35.479 --> 00:03:38.232
It is not simply watching a
tiny upside down photograph

00:03:38.234 --> 00:03:39.665
and physically rotating it.

00:03:40.498 --> 00:03:43.811
If the room gets darker, does
the lens widen the pupil?

00:03:44.586 --> 00:03:46.580
That would make our model eye roll at us.

00:03:47.096 --> 00:03:48.953
The iris changes pupil size.

00:03:49.397 --> 00:03:52.009
Focusing uses the lens
and ciliary muscles.

00:03:52.309 --> 00:03:54.233
Different jobs, different structures.

00:03:54.994 --> 00:03:58.018
For a nearby book, what
happens during accommodation?

00:03:58.772 --> 00:04:02.330
The ciliary muscles contract, the
suspensory ligaments become less

00:04:02.348 --> 00:04:05.480
tense, and the lens becomes
thicker and more curved.

00:04:05.858 --> 00:04:07.358
That gives stronger focusing.

00:04:08.143 --> 00:04:08.700
Correct.

00:04:09.043 --> 00:04:13.075
For distant objects, the ciliary
muscles relax, the ligaments become

00:04:13.075 --> 00:04:15.286
tauter, and the lens becomes flatter.

00:04:15.751 --> 00:04:19.596
Remember that contracting the ciliary
muscles does not pull the lens flatter.

00:04:20.435 --> 00:04:25.358
A sneaky opposite looking relationship:
contracted muscle, slacker ligaments,

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.491
rounder lens for near objects.

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:29.039
Exactly.

00:04:29.312 --> 00:04:33.467
At the point where the optic nerve leaves
the retina, there are no photoreceptors.

00:04:33.777 --> 00:04:35.263
That creates a blind spot.

00:04:35.754 --> 00:04:39.208
Our visual system usually fills in
our experience using surrounding

00:04:39.209 --> 00:04:40.000
information and both eyes.
